Take mdns-sd and if-addrs from crates.io

mdns-sd comes from a git branch ("unsolicited") of a personal fork, not
pinned to a commit, so what is built changes whenever the branch moves,
and it cannot be vendored or audited as a release. The branch is mdns-sd
0.10.4 plus two commits: `register_resend`, used only by the BLE
re-announcement the earlier patch removed, and an `enable_addr_auto`
variant that limits the published addresses to IPv4 or IPv6. 0.10.4 is
from early 2024; the mDNS parser that every packet on the link reaches
has had robustness fixes since.

This uses mdns-sd 0.21 from crates.io. Its enable_addr_auto takes no
argument; the daemon is told to leave IPv6 interfaces alone instead,
which keeps the service to A records as before.

get_if_addrs (0.5, last released in 2017, with its own C shim) is
replaced by if-addrs, its maintained successor and the crate mdns-sd
itself uses, for the one thing the library needs it for: telling our own
addresses from a peer's.

sys_metrics, the other git dependency, goes with the global device name
in the patch that makes the embedding application name the device.
---
 Cargo.toml      |  6 ++++--
 src/hdl/mdns.rs | 11 ++++++++---
 src/utils.rs    |  7 +++----
 3 files changed, 15 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Cargo.toml b/Cargo.toml
index 0e3a915..df78e01 100644
--- a/Cargo.toml
+++ b/Cargo.toml
@@ -13,12 +13,14 @@ base64 = "0.22"
 bytes = "1.10"
 cbc = { version = "0.1", features = ["alloc"] }
 directories = "6.0"
-get_if_addrs = "0.5"
 hex = "0.4"
 hkdf = "0.12"
 hmac = "0.12"
+# Only to tell our own addresses from a peer's; mdns-sd uses the same crate.
+if-addrs = "0.15"
 log = "0.4"
-mdns-sd = { git = "https://github.com/Martichou/mdns-sd", branch = "unsolicited" }
+# crates.io release instead of an unpinned git branch of a fork.
+mdns-sd = "0.21"
 mime_guess = "2.0"
 num-bigint = "0.4"
 once_cell = "1.20"
